{
  "_comment": [
    "Shared Stellar strkey decoder vectors: ONE file read by every hand-written strkey decoder test so they cannot drift.",
    "Readers: contracts/stellar/bindings/go/escrow-encoding (ParseAddress, the single decoder), contracts/stellar/bindings/typescript/escrow-encoding (Address.fromStrkey), schemas/declarations/common/chain-address (Go and TypeScript, which call them), and schemas/declarations/FulfillmentQuote/v1/bindings/{go,typescript}/signing (G accounts only, which also call them).",
    "valid: address (a strkey) decodes to kind (account = G, contract = C) and the 32-byte payloadHex. Re-encoding the kind and payload must give the address back.",
    "invalid: the decoder must refuse. error is the escrow-encoding error class: invalid_strkey (ErrInvalidStrkey / InvalidStrkeyError: malformed, wrong checksum or payload size, non-canonical, or a version that is not an address) or unsupported_address (ErrUnsupportedAddress / UnsupportedAddressError: a well-formed muxed M or claimable-balance B key the contract has no 33-byte form for). Consumers that only accept or reject (chain-address, the quote signer) treat both classes as a rejection.",
    "Cases that embed or surround a newline, carriage return, space or NUL exist because Go's base32 decoder silently drops CR and LF: a decoder that checks length before the alphabet reads 55 characters plus one newline as a 31-byte key. Strings are JSON-escaped, so the control characters are literal here.",
    "The field is named address, not strkey, so the repo secret scanner does not read these public identifiers as API keys. Hand-authored with scratch tooling that builds each strkey from kind and payload with an independent CRC-16/XMODEM; add a case here, not in a language test, so every decoder gets it."
  ],
